Ladybird now gives a space the first font in the font list whose
unicode-range covers it and that has a glyph for it, as for any other
character. Run in Chrome 158, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5, all three
do the same: A space between digits from a digits-only Ahem face takes
the next font’s space, a space between words from a later font takes
the first font’s 1em Ahem space, and spaces after a Japanese word in
monospace text are one column wide. Blink’s HarfBuzzShaper shapes a
run with the primary font, and reshapes only the characters it has no
glyph for with the next font from its FontFallbackIterator. Gecko’s
gfxFontGroup::FindFontForChar() continues the previous character’s font
only for cluster extenders and NNBSP, and walks the font group for a
space. An emoji font listed first still gives way to the first text
font for a space, which keeps baa9b6cc345’s rule.
